---
import QualityLayout from "../../../layouts/QualityLayout.astro";
---

<QualityLayout
  title="Methodology — Counterfact 2026 Quality Audit"
  description="Scope, classification rules, evidence standards, validation, and limitations for Counterfact’s 2026 external bug audit."
  canonicalPath="/quality/2026/methodology"
  active="methodology"
>
  <header class="quality-page-header">
    <div class="quality-wide">
      <p class="quality-crumbs"><a href="/quality/2026">2026 quality audit</a> / Methodology</p>
      <p class="quality-kicker">Scope, rules, and limits</p>
      <h1>How the audit reached its conclusions</h1>
      <p class="quality-deck">
        The classification is based on observable history and release chronology, not the
        language of issue titles, fix pull requests, or assumptions about who wrote the code.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <article class="quality-section quality-reading quality-prose">
    <h2>Population</h2>
    <p>
      The audit compares two identical report windows: January 1 00:00 UTC through September 4
      00:00 UTC, end-exclusive, in 2025 and 2026. It covers public GitHub issues and accepted
      standalone defect pull requests opened by human reporters who were not Counterfact
      organization members or maintainers at the time. Outside contributors remain external.
    </p>
    <p>
      Searches were rerun without relying on labels. Maintainer-authored reports, automated
      bug-hunt issues, enhancements, invalid-input reports, and questions that did not describe
      confirmed faulty behavior were excluded. A pull request tied to a qualifying issue is
      evidence for that issue, not a second report.
    </p>
    <p>
      The final product cohorts contain four 2025 defects and ten 2026 defects. 2026 PR #1516
      is included because it independently reported and fixed faulty behavior without a separate
      issue. Issue #2348 is a process-quality incident: it is analyzed, but excluded from product
      defect percentages because the published code was authentic.
    </p>

    <h2>Classification rules</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Same-year regression:</strong> the same supported behavior demonstrably worked
        before a specific change in the report year and failed afterward.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Defect in a same-year feature:</strong> the applicable capability first appeared
        in the report year and was defective in its initial release.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Pre-existing:</strong> the failure is confirmed before the report year without
        an intervening working state.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The rules are applied in that order. Every confirmed bug receives one primary category.
      Confidence and notes carry uncertainty; the audit does not invent a fourth catch-all class.
    </p>

    <h2>Edge-case policy</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        A 2026 refactor that exposes an older latent defect remains pre-existing unless the
        refactor changed previously correct observable behavior.
      </li>
      <li>
        A missing edge case in a long-standing subsystem is pre-existing when the same input
        would have failed in 2025, even if nobody reported it until 2026.
      </li>
      <li>
        A capability intentionally introduced in 2026 is a feature defect when it never worked
        correctly; it is a regression only if an earlier released version worked.
      </li>
      <li>
        “Regression test” in a fix description means the test prevents recurrence. It does not,
        by itself, date the origin of the bug.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Evidence standard</h2>
    <p>
      Each case began with the report, accepted fix, merge commit, and first release containing
      the fix. The faulty logic was then traced backward using file history, blame, rename-aware
      history, and content searches. Tags and npm publication dates distinguished an internal
      commit from a released behavior.
    </p>
    <p>
      Classification confidence and chronology confidence are recorded separately. High-confidence classifications require at least two corroborating evidence types—for
      example, source ancestry plus a tagged comparison, or code history plus a focused test
      added by the accepted fix. Historical states were compared at the end of 2025, immediately
      around suspected introducing changes, and at the reporter’s version where applicable.
    </p>
    <p>
      “Exact origin” is used only when history identifies the introducing change. “Confirmed
      affected by” is a conservative bound: the linked commit and release demonstrate the defect
      existed by that point, without claiming it began there. The #1933 history uses this bound
      because the 2023 TypeScript conversion preserved an older query shape rather than creating it.
    </p>

    <h2>Maturity sensitivity</h2>
    <p>
      A second calculation keeps only cases whose first confirmed affected release had at least
      90 complete days of observation before its cohort's September 4 cutoff. All fourteen
      product cases meet that threshold, so the sensitivity result is unchanged: zero same-year
      regressions in both cohorts, two 2026 feature defects, and the remaining cases pre-existing.
      This does not make late releases defect-free; it removes them from claims they had too little
      time to support.
    </p>

    <h2>Context metrics</h2>
    <p>
      Release counts come from npm publication timestamps. Pull-request counts come from GitHub’s
      merged-PR search for identical January 1–September 3 windows; the author breakdown is
      reported so dependency automation is visible. Test-file and declaration counts use the
      same filename and source patterns at tag 1.4.7 and the September 3 main commit. Coverage
      percentages are the corresponding public Coveralls results.
    </p>
    <p>
      npm download totals use the registry’s public point API for January 1–August 31 in each
      year. Historical star counts use GitHub’s timestamped stargazer records. These numbers are
      date-qualified because downloads and stars can change after publication.
    </p>

    <h2>Limitations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        External reports are not the complete defect population. Internal findings and unknown,
        unreported bugs are not counted.
      </li>
      <li>
        Fourteen defects are a small sample, and one reporter supplied six of the 2026 issue reports. The distribution
        should not be treated as a universal defect rate.
      </li>
      <li>
        GitHub authorship identifies provenance, not sole responsibility. Contributions pass
        project review, and agent-authored pull requests still require specification, review,
        integration, and release decisions.
      </li>
      <li>
        Pull requests, releases, tests, coverage percentages, downloads, and stars illuminate
        different dimensions; none is a standalone measure of engineering quality.
      </li>
      <li>
        The repository’s 2026 package reorganization changes the coverage denominator. The
        before-and-after percentages are real public results, but not a controlled experiment
        over an identical file set.
      </li>
      <li>
        The absence of an observed 2026 regression does not prove that no such regression exists
        or that any particular development tool caused the observed result.
        It establishes only that none appears in the defined, validated public-report population.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="quality-callout">
      <strong>Reproducible takeaway:</strong> the checked-in evidence manifest records exact
      windows, queries, candidate dispositions, npm timestamps, category inputs, metric sources,
      and rerun commands. The offline verifier recomputes cohort totals, category totals, the
      90-day sensitivity, deduplication invariants, and the pull-request author sum.
    </div>

